Exercise 4.10 asks for a domain \(D_a\) with \(|D_a|=\{x\in|D|\mid x\sqsubseteq a\}\), for the restriction of an approximable endomap \(f\) to an approximable endomap \(f':D_{\mathrm{fix}(f)}\to D_{\mathrm{fix}(f)}\) with \(f'(x)=f(x)\) on that down-set, and for the number of fixed points of \(f'\). `relSystem` keeps the tokens and the master of the original system and takes as neighbourhoods the members of the filter \(a\). `embed` sends a relativised filter to the original element of neighbourhoods containing some member of the filter, `restrict` keeps the membership of an element already under \(a\), `embed_restrict` and `restrict_embed` are the two round trips, `embed_le` lands under \(a\), and `relIso` is the order isomorphism between elements of the relativised system and elements of \(D\) lying under \(a\). `relMap`, available once \(f(a)=a\), relates \(X\) to \(Y\) when \(X\) belongs to \(a\) and \(f\) relates \(X\) to \(Y\); `relMap_toElementMap_embed` says that embedding the image under the restriction recovers the image under \(f\). `fixElement_below_unique` concludes that a fixed point lying under the least fixed point equals it, since a fixed point is a pre-fixed point and the least fixed point is least among those. `relMap_unique_fixed` embeds a fixed point of the restriction, applies that uniqueness, and returns along `restrict_embed`, so every fixed point of the restriction equals the restriction of the least fixed point to itself. `example_410` is the conjunction of those two statements. The development does not prove that this top element is itself a fixed point of `relMap`, so the count is at most one. **Theorem:** partial — \(D_a\), the restriction, and the identity \(f'(x)=f(x)\) through the embedding are constructed, and every fixed point of the restriction equals the top element, while existence of that fixed point is not stated. **Proof:** partial — the round trips and the uniqueness argument through a fixed point below the least fixed point are proved, and the existence half of the count is not.
